WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.717
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
This week, we're

00:00:01.717 --> 00:00:04.380
talking about managing GCP
costs, maps best practices,

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.200
and memory optimized
instances for SAP.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.060
I'm Mark, and welcome
to This Week in Cloud,

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.270
where we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.040
If you manage
Google Cloud costs,

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.332
you'll want to make sure you
check out the new Qwiklabs

00:00:16.332 --> 00:00:18.690
quest, Understanding
Your GCP Costs.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.870
With instructional
videos and hands-on labs,

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:22.620
it's a great way to
learn the fundamentals

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.630
and try out the
GCP tools you can

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:28.380
use to understand, visualize,
and manage your costs.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.890
You can even use the
code 1q-costs-626 to go

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.110
through the labs for free.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.940
Users expect
applications using maps

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.733
to be quick and responsive.

00:00:37.733 --> 00:00:39.150
That's why Travis
is here to share

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.370
some tips on optimizing
your Google Maps platform

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:42.630
applications.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.300
With these best practices, you
can build your app for scale.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.403
Plus, there's even more
tips in the video link.

00:00:48.403 --> 00:00:50.070
If you run any kind
of internet service,

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.110
you know that production
incidents happen.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:54.810
The SRE philosophy is all
about minimizing the impact

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:58.020
of incidents, while also making
sure they're a learning event.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.330
See the details of how a
production incident happens,

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.250
and learn about some
tools to help lower

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:05.040
the number of incidents
with this guide.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.930
You can now deploy
workloads that require up

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:10.560
to 12 terabytes of memory in
a single instance on Google

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:11.760
Compute Engine.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.760
These huge instances are
certified by SAP S/4HANA

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.970
and can make sure that you have
the room you need to scale.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.627
See more about the details, and
then try them out for yourself.

00:01:20.627 --> 00:01:22.710
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.940
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:26.110
Have any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:27.760
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.260
We'll see you in the cloud.

